
bocl> (list-all-packages)

(#<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9db98> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d8f0> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d6c8> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d540>)


Something more like:

(#<Package "SWANK-MACROSTEP"> #<Package "SWANK-REPL"> #<Package "SWANK-TRACE-DIALOG">
 #<Package "SWANK-MREPL"> #<Package "SWANK-API"> #<Package "SWANK-IO-PACKAGE">
 #<Package "SWANK/GRAY"> #<Package "CROSS-REFERENCE"> …)

was expected.

If we define at the bocl REPL:

(defun print-object ((package package) stream)
  (print-unreadable-object (package stream :identity nil :type t)
    (format stream "~S" (package-name package)))
  package)

bocl> (setq *print-readably* nil)
NIL
bocl> (list-all-packages)

(#<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9db98> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d8f0> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d6c8> #<PACKAGE 0xc5ff9d540>)


We still don't get what we want…

Furthermore:

bocl> (setq *print-readably* t)
T
bocl> (list-all-packages)

should signal a PRINT-NOT-READABLE error like:

Attempt to print object #<Package "SWANK-MACROSTEP"> on stream #<string-output-stream  #x3020023C578D> .
   [Condition of type print-not-readable]



bocl> (mapcar 'package-name (list-all-packages))

("COMMON-LISP" "COMMON-LISP-USER" "KEYWORD" "BOCL")


We may suspect that other type of objects cannot be used to dispatch
CLOS methods such as PRINT-OBJECT. This is a more general bug, not
specific to PACKAGE, but to all lisp objects (kernel) vs
STANDARD-OBJECT. Even if PACKAGE is not a STANDARD-OBJECT, CLOS
methods can be defined to dispatch on them.
